hey y'all it's andrew couch here and  this tire tuesday video  we're not going to be doing any coding  today instead i wanted to offer five  tips for starting your own data science  projects  so i've definitely been getting some  questions about one how i got a data  scientist position  or how i found jobs in data science or  how i even developed some skills in r  and i and the main answer is i just  started a lot of  side projects in r and i truly think  that  um data science projects or just side  project projects in general  will give you some competitive edge to  other  uh candidates especially if you lack  if you lack some type of experience say  you had an  internship revoked during covid or  you're just trying to find an internship  um my first tip  for uh getting a for starting your own  first data science  project is to choose a topic you're  actually interested in  um if you're gonna spend like 40 to 100  hours  uh coding and working on a data science  project or model  you should at least be very you you  should be interested in it  especially since uh you're putting on  your resume you will  be needing to talk about your projects  a lot uh i know when i was doing a lot  of interviewing  i was constantly talking about  the problems i faced in my projects why  i'd even started these projects and what  it actually does  so choosing a topic you're interested in  is an important  aspect of a data science project and  these topics can range from  a lot of things so on one hand it could  be just you're interested in sports so  you want to do a lot of sports analysis  or sports models  or you could be interested in a specific  data science technique such as like  time series analysis or classification  models  it really doesn't matter as long as you  have some type of interest in what  you're trying to do  my next tip for your data science  projects  is make sure that the data you're using  requires some type of cleaning  or some acquisition process  so with kaggle kaggle has really great  data sets  but kaggle should not be used for your  data science projects to be put on your  resume  is kaggle essentially is like the gym  for data scientists  where you're trying to practice and  strengthen your skills  saying like modeling or computer vision  but  when you're out in the on the field  actually doing it in the applied  workplace your data will be more messy  and will require more cleaning  so with my classification model for the  sorting hat project  most of the data came from a package um  but the data was just the entire  text of the harry potter books to  required a lot of data extraction  where i essentially had to extract all  the dialogue from the books and create  labels for the dialogue  which required a lot of regular  expressions and also use of  engrams additionally i have another side  project  uh called my ufc sports betting model  which required  some data scraping so i was i did some  create some functions to scrape some  ufc fights and ufc data  and that was basically how i created my  data sets for  on my ufc sportsbank model that i'm  working on right now  so one thing that's also good about just  like  having your own data acquisition  functions or data scraping functions is  that  you're really able to understand the  data that you're using and you kind of  get a nice  understanding of like what you're trying  to do and  you talk about it more so  my third tip for starting your own data  science projects  is to make sure the project is easily  understood by hr  um with hr they're gonna be the first  ones reading your actual  resume and they'll be seeing your  projects first  and you always have to consider that hr  is not technical so you should really  try to keep the jargon  at a minimum and have some type of high  level summary so that anyone  who reads it will understand what you're  trying to do for example  one of the main reasons why i created a  harry potter soaring hat model  and not just called it like a nlp text  mining model  nlp text model is because everyone knows  what harry potter is  it's a very popular franchise and people  know what the sorting hat does  so when they read harry potter sorting  hat model  you can understand that it's like some  type of model that will determine  what house you belong to right  and what's interesting about it is that  um  most of the time people when i  interviewed would ask me about the harry  potter soaring hat model  over sweet board because the harry  potter story hat model just seemed more  interesting to people who  aren't interested in data science um  which is one of the reasons why i kind  of regret creating sweetboard  is it it it's a little harder to  understand what this  um dashboard actually does but i mostly  created this  dashboard because i was using it for my  own um work just doing a lot of  twitter text analysis so i decided to  actually  um keep up my resume but i would  definitely recommend  to create a project that's easily  understandable  i was actually watching some videos of  you know side projects for software  engineers  um although it's not directly related to  data science  um a lot of the projects were very  convoluted  where you had to have some type of  computer science knowledge  to get understand what they're doing and  i think that can be detrimental to  a lot of resumes because most of times  it's going to be an hr person reading  your resume first and if they don't  understand it  uh they're just gonna throw it out and  it basically wastes  your time because you spent a lot of  time on the project  and it hurts your job prospects  so my fourth tip is to make sure your  project  has a clear meaning clear relationship  with delivering business value  so creating and doing analysis on  something  is great and all or doing something like  correlation or  uh topic modeling on on a data set is  cool  but you need to have it relate to to  business value  and business value is either increasing  revenue or decreasing costs  so for example a harry potter sorting  hat model when a data science  hiring manager or someone technical sees  it they understand that it's basically  an nlp classification model and nlp  classification models or the harry  potter sorting  model can basically be repackaged to  like  a customer churn model that would read  like uh email feedback right  which is pretty easy to understand how  it could be used in the workplace  um if you just uh tweaked a few things  for sweetboard it's essentially a tool  that could uh speed up the process of  doing text mining or nlp  which can basically uh decrease cost by  increasing efficiency  and productivity among like analysts who  are wanting to study some text data  um finally my last tip for starting your  data science project  is when you actually finish your data  science project you should have it  public somewhere  so for example i would recommend that  you just put it on your github  i think github is basically a gold  standard for data science and software  engineering  so just having your repo or your project  on a repo  is important um one thing i would say is  most of the time they won't actually  look at your code but having your github  account on your resume uh can add some  credibility to it  additionally if they do look at it you  want to make sure that you're  documenting well  and you're you're kind of coding with  some best practices  so it's not just a mess you have some  type of thing that  is organized is understandable and it's  readable to anyone who  wants to see it when you're also  showing your projects i would recommend  having some type of visual component to  it  so for example i have a public tableau  account  and i created a dashboard analyzing the  iowa elections  and this actually landed me an  internship and a fellowship  so having some type of visual component  it's just better in general because  people don't really want to see code  they want to see some type of visual  aspect of it  to get us uh so it's easier to get like  a so what  uh or some type of outcome  um i also  uh have my sweetboard on my shiny apps  so there are a lot of  free services that will allow you to  deploy things online  very quickly um so definitely have that  in mind when you're creating a model is  have some type of present  page for it to present your findings or  at least to add some interactiveness to  it  uh let's see here and i think that's  basically it for today  um i wanted to make a video just on data  science projects i'm a huge  advocate for data science projects and i  think the best thing to do  is just start the project keep these  tips in mind  but it's better to start working on it  first and then kind of tweaking it  um i truly think that data science  projects are all very valuable  if anything you'll be able to learn  something new  and hopefully you'll be able to find a  job so i'll see you guys next week and  tidy on

